/** @page pgsqlmutationobserverguide COPY and the Datasource Mutation Observer

    @tableofcontents

    When the driver is built against %Qore 3.0 or later and a
    @ref Qore::SQL::AbstractDatasource "datasource mutation observer" is registered on the datasource or pool, a <tt>COPY ... FROM STDIN</tt> operation
    reports bounded stream boundaries while it streams:

    - a stream begin boundary once the server has entered \c COPY \c IN mode;
    - a stream progress boundary carrying the number of bytes sent so far, delivered on 64 KB
      boundaries rather than per row so that the reporting cost does not scale with the row count;
    - a stream end boundary carrying the total number of bytes sent and whether the stream completed.

    The payload is never buffered merely to measure it: the direct-SQL path serializes and sends one
    row at a time, while the driver-neutral native path serializes the block already buffered by
    BulkSqlUtil and sends it once.  Only cumulative byte counts are retained for reporting.  Consumed
    bytes are the exact number of escaped text-COPY payload bytes handed to libpq, including field
    separators and row terminators.  This can differ from BulkSqlUtil's driver-neutral fallback
    estimate, which measures serialized values without PostgreSQL COPY escaping and framing.  The
    driver cannot know the complete payload size before serializing it, so it reports a declared size
    of 0 and %Qore falls back to the \c "max_growth_bytes" value of the producer's mutation
    declaration, if any.

    A consumer can reject the stream at the begin boundary, in which case no data is sent at all, or
    stop it at any progress boundary, in which case the \c COPY is aborted with \c PQputCopyEnd()
    and the transaction must be rolled back.  In both cases the exception raised by the consumer is
    propagated to the caller.

    @par Example
    @code{.py}
{
    # declare what the operation does; Qore never inspects the SQL to work this out
    SqlMutationDeclaration decl(ds, <SqlMutationInfo>{
        "op_id": op_id,
        "path_id": "my_table.copy",
        "op_code": OP_COPY,
        "effect": SQL_MUTATION_EFFECT_MAY_GROW,
        "max_growth_bytes": 16 * 1024 * 1024,
    });
    int rows = ds.exec("COPY my_table (id, name) FROM STDIN", {
        "id": ids,
        "name": names,
    });
    ds.commit();
}
    @endcode

    @note
    - This requires %Qore 3.0 or later; when built against an older %Qore the driver compiles the
      reporting calls out entirely and the \c COPY path is unchanged
    - Array binds (see @ref pgsqlbulkdmlguide) are sent in a single round trip rather than streamed, so
      they are reported through the ordinary statement and transaction boundaries instead of as a
      bounded stream
*/
